{"records":[{"id":"6s5hn73jcv","title":"Proteomics explains client specificity of the human Sec62/Sec63 complex in ER protein import","description":"In mammalian cells, one-third of all polypeptides are transported into/across the ER-membrane via the Sec61-channel. While the Sec61-complex facilitates translocation of all polypeptides with amino-terminal signal peptides (SP) or transmembrane helices, the Sec61-associated Sec62/Sec63-complex supports translocation of only a subset, i.e. in a substrate-specific manner. To characterize Sec62- and Sec63-dependent precursors, we combined siRNA-mediated Sec62- or Sec63-depletion in human cells, label-free quantitative proteomics, and differential expression analysis. Alternatively, their CRISPR/Cas9-mediated knock-out in HEK 293 cells was carried out. The results were validated by western blotting under similar steady-state conditions and after short-term over-expression and simultaneous proteasome inhibition, respectively. SP analysis of Sec62- and Sec63-clients revealed the distinguishing feature. Thus, Sec62/Sec63-complex acts as an sp-receptor on the ER-membrane’s cytosolic face for certain precursor polypeptides and triggers substrate-specific (and regulated) opening of the Sec61-channel by its interactions with Sec61α. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-03-04T13:32:59.494Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6s5hn73jcv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6s5hn73jcv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Richard","last_name":"Zimmermann","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Stefan","last_name":"Schorr","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Proteomics","Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"wkttx6tp2d","title":"S. aureus transposon sequencing in the murine vaginal tract ","description":"Results from TRANSIT Tn-seq conditional essentiality analysis, associated to the paper \"Genome-wide mutagenesis identifies factors involved in MRSA vaginal colonization \" by Lyon et al. (2025), under review at Cell Reports. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-02-18T22:39:47.454Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/wkttx6tp2d","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/wkttx6tp2d"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Laurie","last_name":"Lyon","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Transposon","Vagina","Mouse Model","Methicillin-Resistant Staphylococcus aureus"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"2v5jg2zksj","title":"TMEM79/MATTRIN defines a pathway for Frizzled regulation and is required for Xenopus embryogenesis","description":"Absctract\nWnt signaling through the Frizzled (FZD) family of serpentine receptors is essential for embryogenesis and homeostasis, and stringent control of the FZD protein level is critical for stem cell regulation. Through CRISPR/Cas9 genome-wide screening in human cells, we identified TMEM79/MATTRIN, an orphan multi-span transmembrane protein, as a specific inhibitor of Wnt/FZD signaling. TMEM79 interacts with FZD during biogenesis and promotes FZD degradation independent of ZNRF3/RNF43 ubiquitin ligases (R-spondin receptors). TMEM79 interacts with ubiquitin-specific protease 8 (USP8), whose activating mutations underlie human tumorigenesis. TMEM79 specifically inhibits USP8 deubiquitination of FZD, thereby governing USP8 substrate specificity and promoting FZD degradation. Tmem79 and Usp8 genes have a pre-bilaterian origin, and Tmem79 inhibition of Usp8 and Wnt signaling is required for anterior neural development and gastrulation in Xenopus embryos. TMEM79 is a predisposition gene for Atopic dermatitis, suggesting deregulation of Wnt/FZD signaling a possible cause for this most common yet enigmatic inflammatory skin disease.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-09-26T05:47:58.606Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/2v5jg2zksj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/2v5jg2zksj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Maorong","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nathalia","last_name":"Amado","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jieqiong","last_name":"Tan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alice ","last_name":"Reis","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mengxu","last_name":"Ge","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jose","last_name":"Abreu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xi","last_name":"He","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Molecular Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"y8dndzn3mg","title":"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication","description":"These data are part of the Supplemental Items associated with the manuscript entitled \"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication\". These files include Supplementary Data file 1-4.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-03-16T10:34:13.579Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y8dndzn3mg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y8dndzn3mg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Limeng","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus","Screening","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9x47wzy8x7","title":"A study of \"Genome-wide CRISPR screens reveal host factors critical for SARS-CoV-2 infection\" by Wei et al. Cell 2020","description":"Identification of host genes essential for SARS-CoV-2 infection may reveal novel therapeutic targets and inform our understanding of COVID-19 pathogenesis. Here, we performed genome-wide CRISPR screens in Vero-E6 cells with SARS-CoV-2, MERS-CoV, bat coronavirus HKU5 expressing the SARS-CoV-1 spike, and VSV expressing the SARS-CoV-2 spike. Together this identifies potential therapeutic targets for SARS-CoV-2 and reveals SARS-lineage specific and pan-coronavirus host factors that regulate susceptibility to highly pathogenic coronaviruses.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-10-15T21:29:14.06Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9x47wzy8x7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9x47wzy8x7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Craig","last_name":"Wilen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR","Middle East Respiratory Syndrome-Related Coronavirus","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hfbhym45fz","title":"CRISPR screening reveals a requirement for ribosome recycling in efficient SARS-CoV-2 programmed ribosomal frameshifting and viral replication. Rehfeld et al.","description":"Supplemental Table S1. Results of SARS-CoV-2 programmed ribosomal frameshifting genome-wide CRISPR screens (MAGeCK analysis).\nSupplemental Table S2. Oligonucleotides.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-01-19T12:17:58.922Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hfbhym45fz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hfbhym45fz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Joshua","last_name":"Mendell","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Frederick","last_name":"Rehfeld","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/0003xa228","name":"Cancer Prevention and Research Institute of Texas","grant_id":"RP220309"},{"id":"https://ror.org/00np6vq88","name":"Welch Foundation","grant_id":"I-1961-20210327"},{"id":"https://ror.org/006w34k90","name":"Howard Hughes Medical Institute","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Oligonucleotide","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"f7btcr6x6d","title":"A non-canonical function of glutathione to suppress ferroptosis via FSP1","description":"Whole genome-wide CRISPR KO screen","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-10-02T11:41:52.925Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/f7btcr6x6d","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/f7btcr6x6d"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"BO","last_name":"CHU","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"835fcfbvhs","title":"Preparation of CRISPR/Cas9 and gRNA plasmids","description":"Human genome engineering has been transformed by the introduction of the CRISPR (clustered regularly interspaced short palindromic repeats)/Cas (CRISPR-associated) system found in most bacteria and archaea. Type II CRISPR/Cas systems have been engineered to induce RNA-guided genome editing in human cells, where small RNAs function together with Cas9 nucleases for sequence-specific cleavage of target sequences. Here we describe the protocol for Cas9-mediated human genome engineering, including construct building and transfection methods necessary for delivering Cas9 and guide RNA (gRNA) into human-induced pluripotent stem cells (hiPSCs) and HEK293 cells. Following genome editing, we also describe methods to assess genome editing effi- ciency using next-generation sequencing and isolate monoclonal hiPSCs with the desired modifications for downstream applications. Keywords: genome engineering • CRISPR • human stem cells","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2016-12-12T12:58:15.307Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/835fcfbvhs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/835fcfbvhs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"George","last_name":"Church","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Experimental Method"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xn3vv2cdnk","title":"High-throughput CRISPR screens to dissect macrophage-Shigella interactions","description":"The positive hits, negative hits, and non-targeting controls of genome-wide CRISPR screens; Supplemental Tables.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-07-23T06:18:30.182Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xn3vv2cdnk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xn3vv2cdnk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yong","last_name":"LAI","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genetic Screening","Host-Pathogen Interaction","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nxvdzybwhf","title":"Illuminating host-mycobacterial interactions with genome-wide CRISPR knockout and CRISPRi screens","description":"The data deposited here contains genome-wide CRISPR knockout and CRISPRi screen data, including the positive hits, the negative hits, and non-targeting controls. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-08-30T11:32:01.108Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nxvdzybwhf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nxvdzybwhf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yong","last_name":"Lai","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Functional Genomics","High-Throughput Sequencing","Tuberculosis"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":111849,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":107,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":111849,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":111331,"restricted":56,"embargoed":462},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":1944},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":594},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":434},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":374},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":371},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Supe